## 4. Security map

### 4.1 Security principle

Do not claim perfect security.

Claim:

```text
scoped access
clear trust boundaries
encrypted storage where appropriate
approval gates
audit trail
kill switch
revocation
least privilege
```

This is credible. “100% secure” is how amateurs summon lawsuits.

### 4.2 Important truth about hardware ownership

There are two cases.

#### Company-provided VPS/hardware

Empyralis owns the machine.

User should not see:

```text
SSH
filesystem
raw cookies
Telegram session string
WhatsApp auth files
container internals
platform secrets
```

User should see:

```text
status
region
connected channels
health
activity
audit
start/stop/pause/revoke
redacted logs
usage/cost
```

This can be made secure enough for pilot if isolated correctly.

#### User-provided VPS/hardware

User owns the machine.

Reality:

```text
If the user owns root/admin, the user can inspect files.
```

Therefore:

```text
Do not put platform master secrets on user hardware.
Only put workspace-scoped gateway credentials and local session material needed for that user's own work.
```

This protects Empyralis from the user's machine.

It does not hide all runtime internals from that user. That is not how computers work, despite decades of executives trying to negotiate with physics.

### 4.3 Recommended hosted security architecture

For company-provided hosted gateways:

```text
one workspace per gateway/container
one persistent volume per gateway
one gateway_id per runtime
one device_id per runtime
short-lived WebSocket session tokens
gateway token scoped to one gateway/workspace
no SSH/customer shell
no platform master secrets in container
no cross-tenant filesystem
no shared browser profile
no shared channel auth directory
strict resource limits
redacted logs only
kill switch
revocation
activity ledger
```

### 4.4 Session storage choice

MVP:

```text
gateway_local persistent volume
```

Meaning:

```text
Telegram/WhatsApp sessions are stored on the selected gateway's persistent volume.
If container restarts, session should survive.
If the volume is destroyed, user reconnects.
```

Later:

```text
hosted_encrypted_backup
cloud_session_vault
```

Do not build the session vault first.

### 4.5 What to encrypt

MVP:

```text
provider/VPS volume encryption
file permissions 0600
no secret logs
no secret frontend exposure
```

Later:

```text
envelope-encrypted session blobs
per-workspace keys
separate key references
object storage encrypted blobs
audit for restore/export/delete
BYOK for enterprise
```

### 4.6 Required security features before serious pilot

```text
workspace match enforcement
revocation enforcement
fresh heartbeat requirement
device trust status
gateway process lock
cloud-side personal channel lease
outbound idempotency
audit events
redaction tests
kill switch
safe mode
approval rules for risky actions
production auth-disabled rejection
```

### 4.7 Main hacking risks

```text
stolen gateway token
stolen Telegram session string
stolen WhatsApp auth directory
workspace isolation bug
user-owned VPS leaking platform secrets
prompt injection from inbound customer messages
duplicate gateway causing duplicate replies
provider ban/session invalidation
unredacted logs
misconfigured production auth
supply-chain dependency compromise
```

### 4.8 Main mitigations

```text
least-privilege gateway tokens
short-lived session tokens
token rotation and revocation
workspace-scoped runtime identity
tenant/workspace isolation tests
secret redaction
no platform master secrets on gateways
gateway lease/fencing
idempotency key for outbound messages
manual approval for risky actions
per-channel autonomy modes
container isolation
resource limits
non-root runtime where possible
CI secret scanning
dependency audit
SBOM generation
audit trail
emergency stop
```

